If you want to run a Unix command every N seconds forever without drift, use an interval scheduler that sleeps until the next absolute deadline, not “sleep N after the command finishes”.

A simple Perl solution is:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use Time::HiRes qw(time sleep);

sub launch {
    return if fork;
    exec @_;
    die "Couldn't exec";
}

$SIG{CHLD} = 'IGNORE';

my $interval = shift;
my $start = time();

while (1) {
    launch(@ARGV);
    $start += $interval;
    sleep($start - time());
}
```

Example:

```sh
perl timer.pl 1 date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'
```

Why this works:
- It keeps a target schedule in `$start`.
- After each run, it increments `$start` by the interval.
- It sleeps only the remaining time until that target, so execution time doesn’t accumulate as drift.

Notes:
- This is much better than plain `sleep 1`, `watch`, or a loop like `cmd; sleep 1`, which all drift.
- It still can’t be perfectly exact if the system is heavily loaded or `fork/exec` takes too long.
- For sub-second precision or hard real-time guarantees, you’d need a C program or an RTOS/hardware timer approach.

