A device is usually “specified” to Xorg by the input driver through its Linux input properties, not by a special Xorg setting.

From the resolved thread you provided, the key issue was that the virtual mouse device did not expose enough identifying information, so Xorg ignored it as:

No identifier specified, ignoring this device

To make Xorg recognize it, set the input device fields properly in your kernel module before registering the device:

```c
vms_input_dev->name = "Virtual Mouse";
vms_input_dev->phys = "vmd/input0";   // example physical path
vms_input_dev->id.bustype = BUS_VIRTUAL;
vms_input_dev->id.vendor  = 0x0000;
vms_input_dev->id.product = 0x0000;
vms_input_dev->id.version = 0x0000;

vms_input_dev->evbit[0] = BIT_MASK(EV_KEY) | BIT_MASK(EV_REL);
vms_input_dev->keybit[BIT_WORD(BTN_MOUSE)] = BIT_MASK(BTN_LEFT) |
                                             BIT_MASK(BTN_RIGHT) |
                                             BIT_MASK(BTN_MIDDLE);
vms_input_dev->keybit[BIT_WORD(BTN_MOUSE)] |= BIT_MASK(BTN_SIDE) |
                                              BIT_MASK(BTN_EXTRA);
vms_input_dev->relbit[0] = BIT_MASK(REL_X) | BIT_MASK(REL_Y) |
                           BIT_MASK(REL_WHEEL);
```

In short:
- give the device a `name`
- set `phys`
- set a valid `id`
- advertise proper `EV_KEY`, `EV_REL`, button, and motion capabilities

