If your file is whitespace-separated and organized in blocks like the example, the Perl solution from the resolved thread is a good fit.

It reads each block, transposes the columns into rows, and then sorts by the first value.

Perl script:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/env perl

use strict;
use warnings;

my %final_lines;
my $filename = shift // die "No input file given!\n";
open my $IN, '<', $filename or die "Failed to open $filename for input: $!\n";

{
    local $/ = "";   # paragraph mode
    while (<$IN>) {
        my @lines  = split /\n/;
        my @fields = split /\s+/, shift @lines;

        my $line_number = 0;
        for my $line (@lines) {
            my @data = split /\s+/, $line;
            map {
                $final_lines{$fields[$_]}->[$line_number] += $data[$_]
            } (0 .. $#data);
            $line_number++;
        }
    }
}

for my $key (sort { $a <=> $b } keys %final_lines) {
    local $, = ' ';
    print $key, @{$final_lines{$key}}, "\n";
}
```

Run it like this:

```sh
perl transpose.pl input_file > output_file
```

Notes:
- It uses paragraph mode (`local $/ = ""`) to read blank-line-separated blocks.
- It transposes each block into rows.
- Then it sorts the rows numerically by the first column.

