If you want to turn rows into columns, `awk` is usually the easier tool than `sed`.

If your data is a simple CSV-like file with no embedded commas/newlines in fields, this works:

```sh
awk -F, '{
  for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++)
    a[NR, i] = $i
  if (NF > max) max = NF
}
END {
  for (i = 1; i <= max; i++) {
    for (j = 1; j <= NR; j++) {
      printf "%s%s", a[j, i], (j < NR ? "," : "\n")
    }
  }
}' file
```

Example input:

```text
a,b,c
d,e,f
g,h,i
```

Output:

```text
a,d,g
b,e,h
c,f,i
```

If you want tab-separated output instead of CSV, replace `","` with `"\t"` in the `printf`.

If you need to handle real CSV with quoted commas, `awk`/`sed` gets messy quickly. In that case, use a CSV-aware tool or parser.

If your HDD light blinks when memory is exhausted, that usually means the system is doing disk I/O because it’s under memory pressure — but it doesn’t necessarily mean you have swap configured.

A few possibilities:

- It’s paging through the filesystem cache
- It’s writing logs / temp files / buffers
- The kernel is reclaiming memory and touching disk-backed pages
- You may still have swap enabled somewhere else than you expect

To check whether swap is actually configured, use:

```bash
free -m
swapon -s
cat /proc/swaps
```

If those show nothing under Swap, then you really have no swap active.

If you want to test swapping behavior, you can enable swap and then create memory pressure. For example:

1. Check/set swappiness:
```bash
sysctl vm.swappiness
sysctl vm.swappiness=100
```

2. Run a memory eater program or a tool like `stress` / `stress-ng` to consume RAM.

Example simple test program:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    int max = -1;
    int mb = 0;
    int multiplier = 1;
    char* buffer;

    if(argc > 1)
        max = atoi(argv[1]);

    while((buffer = malloc(multiplier * 1024*1024)) != NULL && mb != max) {
        memset(buffer, 1, multiplier * 1024*1024);
        mb++;
        printf("Allocated %d MB\n", multiplier * mb);
        sleep(1);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Compile and run:

```bash
gcc filename.c -o memeater
./memeater
```

That said: if your machine truly has no swap, then once RAM is exhausted the kernel will try to reclaim caches and eventually the OOM killer may terminate processes. In that case the disk light blinking is not “swapping” — it’s just disk activity caused by memory pressure.

