If you’re already in FTP, plain `ls`/`dir` usually won’t show directory sizes, because FTP listings generally report sizes for files only.

To get the disk usage of a directory and everything under it, you need to sum the sizes of all files recursively. One way is to do it with a script using FTP commands.

A Perl example using `Net::FTP` and `File::Listing`:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use Net::FTP;
use File::Listing qw(parse_dir);

my $param = $ARGV[0];

sub getRecursiveDirListing {
    my $ftp = Net::FTP->new("fftpserver", Debug => 0) or die("Cannot connect $!");
    $ftp->login("username", "password") or die("Login failed $!");

    local @dirs = ();
    my $dir = $_[0];

    $ftp->cwd($dir) if ($dir);

    @ls = $ftp->ls('-lR');
    my $cur_dir = $ftp->pwd();

    my $totsize = 0;
    my $i = 0;
    my @arr = parse_dir(\@ls);
    my $arrcnt = scalar(@arr);

    if ($arrcnt == 0) {
        print "$cur_dir 0\n";
        $ftp->quit();
        exit 1;
    } else {
        foreach my $file (parse_dir(\@ls)) {
            $i++;
            my ($name, $type, $size, $mtime, $mode) = @$file;
            $totsize += $size if ($type eq 'f');

            print "$cur_dir $totsize\n" if ($i == $arrcnt);
        }
    }

    $ftp->quit();
}

getRecursiveDirListing($param);
```

Notes:
- This sums only file sizes, not filesystem blocks or overhead.
- FTP itself does not usually provide a true “disk usage” equivalent to `du`.
- If you have shell access to the server, the better command is:

```sh
du -sh /home/directory
```

That gives the actual disk usage of the directory tree.

