If you want a script to run automatically when you enter a directory in bash, the usual approach is to hook into `cd`/`pushd`/`popd` and then run logic based on `$PWD`.

Example:

```bash
cd () { builtin cd "$@" && chpwd; }
pushd () { builtin pushd "$@" && chpwd; }
popd () { builtin popd "$@" && chpwd; }

unset_all_project_settings () {
  # undo anything projectSettings.bash did:
  # unset variables, remove PATH entries, etc.
}

chpwd () {
  case "$PWD" in
    /some/directory|/some/other/directory)
      . ./projectSettings.bash
      ;;
    *)
      unset_all_project_settings
      ;;
  esac
}
```

Important warning: only do this for directories you explicitly trust/whitelist. Otherwise it’s easy to execute arbitrary code just by changing into a directory someone else gave you.

A safer pattern is to use a dedicated helper function instead of auto-running on every `cd`:

```bash
myproj () {
  cd /some/directory && . ./projectSettings.bash
}
```

That way the script only runs when you intentionally enter the project.

