`top` is not contradictory; it’s just reporting CPU usage from a sampled, scheduler-based view.

What it means:

- On a dual-core system, 100% in `top` typically means one core is fully busy.
- A single-threaded process can only run on one core at a time, so it usually won’t exceed 100% in the default view.
- Values like 97%, 99%, or 101% can happen because `top` samples over time and the OS is constantly scheduling other work.

Why you may never see a perfect 100%:

1. Your process is briefly preempted by the OS.
2. `top` is an approximation over a sampling interval.
3. Some CPU time is spent in kernel/interrupt/system work, which may not show exactly as user-process time.

Also, your test code has undefined behavior:

```c
int i;
while (1) {
    i = i + 1;
}
```

`i` is uninitialized. Use:

```c
volatile int i = 0;
while (1) {
    i++;
}
```

If you want wraparound behavior, use an unsigned type, since signed overflow in C is undefined.

So the short answer is: `top` is not missing CPU; it’s just reporting an approximate per-process usage number, not a perfect accounting of every cycle.

