`top` does not measure CPU like a simple “max out at 100%” meter. It shows CPU time as reported by the operating system, so values can look a little odd.

Why you might see 97% instead of 100%:

- `top` updates periodically and rounds the numbers.
- The OS spends some CPU time on scheduling, kernel work, interrupts, and context switches.
- On multi-core systems, CPU usage may be reported per core or averaged in a way that isn’t always intuitive.
- Other system activity can use a small amount of CPU even if your program is the main load.

So if your process is doing a busy loop, it can still be effectively using all available CPU even if `top` shows 97% or 99%.

Also, if your code is:

```c
int main() {
    int i = 0;
    while (1) {
        i++;
    }
}
```

the `int` overflow is not what’s causing the CPU usage behavior. It just means `i` will eventually wrap around. In C, signed integer overflow is undefined behavior, so the compiler may optimize the loop in unexpected ways.

If you want a simple reliable CPU-burning loop for testing, use:

```c
int main() {
    volatile unsigned long i = 0;
    while (1) {
        i++;
    }
}
```

